<speak>
<p>Our matrix has order four u squared, with u odd. We will relabel its cycle using two coordinates. Send position j to j modulo four, and j modulo u squared. These are two residue dials. In the illustration, u equals three, so their lengths are four and nine. Position one becomes one, one. Position four becomes zero, four. Position nine becomes one, zero. Each step advances both dials together.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Why does this lose no positions? Suppose two positions have the same pair. Their difference is divisible by four and by u squared. These lengths are coprime, so their difference is divisible by four u squared. They were the same position on the original cycle. The two descriptions also have the same number of positions. In our example, all thirty six positions fill a four by nine grid, with each cell used exactly once.<break time="700ms"/>Addition is preserved too: four plus nine is thirteen, and their coordinate pairs add to one, four. In general, add each coordinate modulo its own length. Thus the cyclic group of order four u squared is isomorphic to the product of these two cyclic groups.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now translate the polynomial. Introduce Z for the four cycle and P for the u squared cycle. The generator X maps to Z times P. Therefore X to the j becomes Z to the j times P to the j, with the exponents reduced separately. Keep the same coefficient h sub j attached throughout. Every sign moves to exactly one new label; no signs are added or changed.<break time="700ms"/>Multiplication respects addition of exponents. Inversion negates both coordinates, so the star operation is preserved as well. Consequently the original norm identity becomes h of Z, P times h star of Z, P equals four u squared. The new relations are Z to the fourth equals one, and P to the u squared equals one. We have changed coordinates, while keeping the same sign data and the same norm identity.<break time="1100ms"/></p>
</speak>
